that whole issue  of race was crucial to that this was a  moment when the anti-apartheid movement  in South Africa was just beginning and  leaders of that movement came to Rome to  make their case that South Africa should  either end its segregation or be removed  from the Olympic field because it was  breaking the creed of no discrimination  based on race or nationality or religion  or anything the leader of that movement  Denis Brutus when he wrote his letters  to the president of the IOC Avery  Brundage his offices in Pretoria were  ransacked by the South African internal  police when he tried to get a passport  to come to Rome he was denied a passport  eventually one of his cohorts from Great  Britain did come to Rome to make the  case against South Africa presented it  at a meeting of the International  Olympic Committee also there was the  white leader of the all-white South  African Olympic team who made the  argument that there was no  discrimination going on there were no  blacks on the South African team because  no blacks were good enough athletes to  be on the team this was night this was  the world of 1960 and it wasn't until  four years later that eventually the  world caught up and South Africa was  forced to either integrate or leave the  Olympic movement which it did until  apartheid fell on Nelson Mandela came  many many decades later but there was  also that summer a beautiful thing that  happened the Olympics that had to do  with Africa and race to that moment no  black African it ever won a gold medal  and tell a bevvy Bakula a bevvie Makela  came with a delegation of 12 athletes  from Ethiopia mostly runners a few  cyclists nobody had ever heard of him  before the press  or had his name as makyla a bevvy he  arrived with a pair of running shoes  that didn't fit he threw them away as  the marathon the great ending race of  the Olympics was about to begin on the  Capitoline steps in ancient Rome at  American marathoner named Gordon  McKenzie turned to his teammate after  looking over and seeing this little guy  number 11 all sticks and bones  and no shoes turned to his teammate and  said well there's one guy we're not  gonna have to worry about and nobody did  it tell halfway through the race when  word filtered back that a bevy maquila  was in the lead and it was a truly  amazing scene the marathon was held in  the darkness because of the heat of Rome  it had left ancient Rome and went out  past the outer ring of the city and then  the last 12 miles were run along the old  Appian Way in the darkness with men  dressed as Roman soldiers holding  torches and here came this barefoot  Ethiopian illuminated by the torches  running through Rome the capital city of  the nation that had invaded his homeland  Ethiopia only 25 years earlier under the  fascist Mussolini army and injected  mustard gas into the villages around  Addis Ababa a horrendous moment of  Mussolini trying to establish a colonial  empire and here came this one Ethiopia  and as the Ethiopians would say it took  a million-man army to conquer Ethiopia  and one barefoot Ethiopian to conquer  Rome and here he came back  [Applause]  running through the streets barefoot as  he enters the ancient city walls again  he passes the obelisk of Aksum which was  a work of antiquity from Ethiopia that  Mussolini's army had dismantled and  shipped back and set up as the symbol of  their colonial empire in Rome the  barefoot Ethiopian passes the obelisk  and takes off and he finished at the  arch of Constantine illuminated in the  darkness in the fastest time ever for a  marathon an Olympic history a barefoot  Ethiopian the first ever black African  to win a gold medal the first in that  long illustrious line of West Africans  when we think of distance running now we  think of Kenya and Ethiopia a bevy of  Aquila was the first in the whole milieu  of 1960 America race was an incredible  thing going on there were boycotts of  department stores throughout the  American South that summer protests at  lunch counter sit-ins during the  Olympics themselves there was a race  riot in Jacksonville Florida the United  States presenting itself to the world as  a beacon of Liberty was in an  embarrassing moment how did they deal  with it one of the ways they dealt with  it was through the greatest athlete of  the American team Rafer Johnson Rafer  Johnson was a decathlete from UCLA the  decathlon for those of you don't know is  probably the most trying test of  athletic skill imaginable ten events in  two days everything ranging from  Sprint's to the high jump to the pole  vault to the shotput Raiford Johnson was  such a great athlete probably along with  Jim Brown than a football player and  lacrosse player the two greatest  athletes in in the in ameri  he was also the student body president  at UCLA a terrific person in every  respect with great character bearing and  athletic skills he was the captain of  the United States team and he was chosen  to carry the American flag as the US  delegation walked into the Stadio  Olimpico on August 25th 1960 at historic  moment the first African American ever  to be the flag bearer for the US team in  that summer of tension in the Civil  Rights Movement Rafer Johnson was in a  very difficult situation  he knew that he deserved that honor he  was the greatest athlete he was revered  by all of his teammates and by athletes  around the world only two years earlier  in Moscow at the first ever duel meet  between the Soviets and the American  track and field teams Rafer Johnson had  defeated the great Soviet Vasiliy  Kuznetsov and after the final event at  Lenin Stadium this massive Russian  throng surrounded him at first he was  frightened then they lifted and wasted  him onto their shoulders and carried him  out of Lenin Stadium chanting his name  that's how revered Rafer Johnson was he  deserved the honor of carrying the flag  but he also understood that to a certain  extent he was being exploited 1960  America it's different than 1968 all of  us remember the famous incident of  Mexico City where Tommie Smith and John  Carlos stuck their hands up and a Black  Power salute on the medal stand 1960 was  somewhat different  Rayford Johnson knew that he was being  exploited but also that he deserved the  honor he had to make a tough decision  what do I do  he chose he said to himself I will  behave with the class and dignity and  perseverance that I've always shown and  I'll win the gold medal it was easier  for him to do the first in the second  the gold medal contest  was very difficult because his best  friend  and UCLA teammate CK Yan represented  Taiwan at those Olympics and they were  both terrific athletes  the only reason Taiwan competed in those  Olympics was because of CK the State  Department the United States wanted them  to boycott the Olympics rather than  succumb to the IOC demand that they call  themselves Taiwan rather than the  Republic of China politics is always  going on at the Olympics in every  different fashion it was that as much as  now seek a participant participated  because his country knew that he could  win a medal before the final event of  the 11th of the decathlon it was a  brutal competition they were very close  CK was better at the Sprint's Rafer  Johnson was better at them at the  shotput of the discus it came down to  the last event the metric mile they're  both completely exhausted they'd  expended all of their energy for two  days and nine events Rafer Johnson was  slightly in the lead in total points C  Cain was a better miler all he had to do  was finish by 10 seconds better than  Rafer Johnson in that final event and he  would win the gold medal they had the  same coach the UCLA coach ducky Drake  there was in the Stadio Olimpico over in  the first row of the stands over to the  side before the event Rafer Johnson  walked over to CK I mean to ducky Drake  for some advice CK's a better miler I've  got to win this what what should I do  ducky Drake the coach said while CK is a  better miler but you just have to hang  in there and when the final lap comes to  lab at the bell lab CK is going to turn  around and you have to be right at his  shoulder and you'll break his will rafer  stood okay went back started warming up  CK yang walked over to ducky Drake  for advice what do you suggest coach  you're a better mile or CK ravers going  to try to stick with you but it gets to  the last lap turned around and see where  he is the takeoff you'll break his will  ducky Drake was like a great chess  player playing both sides  Rayford Johnson was not a great miler  but he had one terrific advantage he  knew that this was the last time he  would ever compete in the decathlon and  run the mile and he hated the mile so as  the event started he kept saying to  himself this is the last time I'll ever  have to run this blankety-blank  mile I can't stand it just this one last  time I've got to hang in there never  again this is it I'll go on with my life  I just have to deal with this one more  time  he kept him going they got to the last  lap sure enough CK turned around and  there was Rafer Johnson right at his  shoulder smiling he told me he was  smiling out of pain and acting but it  served its purpose CK tried to take off  couldn't stretch out and Rafer Johnson  finish close enough to win that magical  gold medal and as they reached the  finish line in the dusk and drizzle of  that night in Rome they collapsed in  each other's arms and there's an iconic  photograph of them holding each other up  at that moment these two best friends  who'd adored everything they could as  athletes completely exhausted hugging  each other holding each other up and  it's that really all too seldom seen  moment of what the Olympic ideal should  be in robe with Rafer Johnson ina closed  with one final story which means the  most to me and that has to do with Wilma  Rudolph and the tiger bells of Tennessee  State Wilma Rudolph for those of you  don't know won the hundred meter dash  the 200 or - and that was the anchor  of the relay team in Rome she was the  belle of the Olympics the first  televised Olympics this incredibly  willowy agile runner with a charisma and  grace  everybody in Rome was madly in love with  her John Kennedy the president to be  would later invite her to the White  House and fall off his rocking chair in  the Oval Office he was so smitten by her  Cash's play the young eighteen-year-old  boxer why haven't even talked about who  won a gold medal in Rome was  head-over-heels for Rome for Wilma she  had no time for him  Livio Baroody the great Italian sprinter  told the Oh David if only I could have  gotten alone with Wilma I would have  consummated our platonic relationship  everybody loved Wilma but her story is  greater she was one of 22 children from  Clarksville Tennessee her mother was a  maid her father was a porter of the  railroad when she was young she had  scarlet fever and polio she wore leg  braces for three years  every Friday her mother would take off  work and ride a Greyhound bus with her  from Clarksville down to Nashville to go  to Meharry Medical School the one black  teaching trading hospital in the country  or she would get treatments she overcame  that to become a great basketball player  at Byrd High School in Clarksville and a  sprinter she was recruited to the track  team that won a bronze medal in  Melbourne in 1956 the the relay team at  age fairly 16 she came back to high  school was the heroine of her high  school and got pregnant she was a  teenage mother at age 17 overcame all of  that to win those gold medals in Rome  and become the shining star of those  Olympics and representing not just the  United States but all women thereafter  if there is a Mount Rushmore of women  athletes Wilma Rudolph deserves a place  on that Mount Rushmore and so do all of  her Tiger Belles Tennessee State a small  historically black school and North  Nashville Tennessee  in an era long before title 9 there were  no scholarships for women it was  complete Jim Crow segregation in the  south the coach Edie temple didn't even  have his own office  he had a sharin office with his wife was  the postmistress of the school they had  no transport means of transportation  they'd have to take a clunky old station  wagon to compete against Tuskegee  Institute or Alabama state driving  deeper into the deep south of that era  afraid to stop at night they would pack  peanut butter jelly sandwiches they had  to go to the bathroom the women would  shout from the back of the station wagon  time to hit the fields out of that that  nothingness came the greatest women's  track and field program in history  starting with Wilma Rudolph in 1960  winning more medals than any other place  in the United States truly not  recognized the way they should be not  getting their due until I hope this book  now I'll end with one Wilma Rudolph's  story after the Rome Olympics of course  everybody wanted to see her as I said  John Kennedy invite her to the White  House  and her hometown of Clarksville wanted  to honor her with a banquet and a parade  and barely 20 years old in 1960 America  after all she'd overcome Wilma Rudolph  had the guts to say no not unless the  events are integrated never before  happened that one day and night in  Clarksville Tennessee in 1960 black and  white officials black and white families  sat together in the armory of  Clarksville to honor Wilma Rudolph and  in that one small way she did change the  world thank you very much  I wonder what question over on your  right oh okay got it  how well do you think China fulfills its  promises to let protesters to to let the  Olympics be open compared to what they  actually did and how how could america  influence them to follow through on  their promises  you know before those Olympics started I  said that would be the story of those  Olympics how China handled it handle